  "quantification": "PE reports 60.462M mapped participant persons / 66.364M annual eligible persons = 91.107%; Urban reports 57.5% and a rounded 29.361M gap. Dividing FNS average-month persons by PE's annual eligible pool gives 63.55% for FY2023 and 62.82% for FY2024. That substitution removes 27.55-28.29 percentage points, or 82.0-84.2% of the published 33.61-point rate difference, leaving 6.05 points (FY2023) or 5.32 points (FY2024). PE's mapped participant count is 43.4-45.0% above FNS, while Urban's participant count inferred from its rounded gap is 4.6-5.7% below FNS. PE eligibility is only 4.0% below Urban.",
  "fix_draft": {
    "type": "annotation",
    "title": "SNAP person rates mix annual SPM-unit membership with average-month FNS persons",
    "body": "PolicyEngine's 91.1% is a derived annual person statistic: every person in an SPM unit with positive 2024 SNAP is counted. Build P consumes USDA FNS average-month household targets, so household agreement is calibration-by-construction; FNS person targets are deliberately excluded because PE does not model within-SPM SNAP assistance-unit membership. FNS FY2023/FY2024 average-month persons divided by PE's annual eligible pool imply 63.6%/62.8%, leaving 6.1/5.3 percentage points relative to Urban's 57.5%. Do not present 91.1% and 57.5% as like-for-like rates."
  },
  "open_questions": [
    "Why does Urban's participant count inferred from its rounded gap sit 1.92-2.41M below the FNS FY2024/FY2023 national person series?",
    "How much of the remaining difference comes from average-month eligibility versus PE's annual eligible pool?",
    "Should PE model SNAP assistance-unit membership instead of mapping the SPM-unit flag to all members?"
  ]
}

